@[disk-partition step]
First, create the partitions to install SMGL to. To see your disks, their
partitions and filesystems, use @[lsblk -f] (or @[lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL]).
Disks are named /dev/nvme0n1 (NVMe), /dev/sda (SATA, USB), /dev/mmcblk0
(SD/eMMC) or /dev/vda (virtual machines); partitions /dev/nvme0n1p1, /dev/sda1.

How this machine booted the ISO decides what you need:
 @[ls /sys/firmware/efi]  # exists: UEFI; "No such file": BIOS
 UEFI: a GPT disk with an EFI system partition (FAT32, 512 MiB is plenty;
       reuse an existing one when keeping another OS) plus a root partition
 BIOS: an MBR (dos) disk with a root partition, or GPT plus a 1 MiB
       "BIOS boot" partition (no filesystem) for GRUB

Partition with any of these:
 @[cfdisk /dev/sda]  menus (choose gpt or dos for an empty disk)
 @[fdisk /dev/sda]   interactive commands (m for help)
 @[parted /dev/sda]  interactive or one-line commands
 @[sfdisk /dev/sda]  scriptable; e.g. a whole disk for UEFI (erases it!):
   @[printf 'label: gpt\nsize=512MiB, type=uefi\ntype=linux\n' | sfdisk /dev/sda]

Swap is optional: a swap partition, or a swap file later. Remember to write
the new partition table before quitting the program. For help choosing a
layout, a filesystem and swap: @[guide disks].

Type @[next] when you're done.
